Darling implements an image-backed subset of DiskArbitration through the
darling-dmg HFS+/HFSX reader. Only a producer retaining a successfully opened
regular image publishes media; arbitrary host mounts are not discovered as disks.

Image media have stable producer-lifetime identities, whole/volume descriptions,
and real mount paths while mounted. They are not BSD block devices:
DADiskGetBSDName returns NULL and no IOKit media object is published for them.
A handle created from a BSD name alone does not establish that media exists.

Mount/eject requests run asynchronously and complete on the session's scheduled
runloop or dispatch queue. Description and disappearance callbacks retain their
event snapshots. Unscheduling pauses delivery without consuming queued events.
Mount arguments are limited to the reader's read-only capabilities; unsupported
options return errors rather than granting writable access.

Unmount approvals are consulted across scheduled sessions and processes in the
same prefix, including hdiutil detach. Each voter runs on its scheduled context;
missing, incomplete or timed-out decisions never become implicit consent.
Mounted state is checked against the actual kernel mount ID and producer source.

tests/image_backend.c exercises real attach, symlink mount paths, payload bytes,
callback pause/resume, separate-session and hdiutil vetoes, and actual eject.
